  "validate": "H158",
  "narrative": "The new discovery evidence gives a useful screen-depth stress test for the four core within-dataset associations. In high screen_nnmd subsets, all four core contrasts remain strongly supported in the registered direction. H155/R94 shows a large, precise signed effect of +0.761 for SMARCA4 loss versus SMARCA2 dependency, meaning stronger SMARCA2 dependency in SMARCA4-loss cells; H156/R95 gives +0.793 for KEAP1 loss versus NFE2L2 dependency under the +1 direction, meaning weaker NFE2L2 dependency in KEAP1-loss cells; H157/R96 gives +0.791 for MSI-high versus WRN dependency, meaning stronger WRN dependency in MSI-high cells; and H158/R97 gives +0.370 for TP53 wild-type status versus USP7 dependency, meaning stronger USP7 dependency in TP53 wild-type cells. These magnitudes are far larger than the small null associations observed for low-prior predictors, so despite multiple testing the core acceptances are not plausibly explained by weak screen_nnmd subsets or technical screening depth. I accept H155-H158. I close H155-H157 because they are clear subset replications, but leave H158 active and request validation because its effect is smaller and the USP7 association remains the core effect with the most context-specific uncertainty; an independent high screen_nnmd replication would be informative.\n\nThe aneuploidy interactions are mostly negative results. H159-H161 are near-zero interactions for SMARCA2, NFE2L2, and WRN, with signed estimates -0.017, -0.003, and +0.022, and intervals that do not indicate substantial effect modification. I reject these registered directional interaction hypotheses and close them, interpreting the evidence as no meaningful context dependence on high versus low aneuploidy score for these three core effects. H162 is less certain: the USP7 aneuploidy interaction is signed -0.077, with a wide interval that could accommodate a moderate attenuation of the USP7 effect in high-aneuploidy cells. I therefore leave H162 unresolved and active rather than rejecting it, because the estimate is near zero but the uncertainty is too large to confidently exclude a modest context effect.\n\nThe RTK/MAPK pathway-bypass hypotheses H163-H166 are also unsupported. PIK3CA mutation versus ERBB2 dependency (H163/R102), KRAS mutation versus EGFR dependency (H164/R103), NF1 loss versus EGFR dependency (H165/R104), and KRAS mutation versus BRAF dependency (H166/R105) all yield near-zero signed estimates with intervals centered on null and small relative to the core dependencies. I reject these claims as registered directional hypotheses. This does not prove absence of any biological relationship; rather, in this dataset these measured predictors are not associated with scientifically meaningful differences in the tested dependency scores. These conclusions remain within-dataset associations and do not establish causality or external generalizability.",
  "research_notes": "Core accepted within-dataset associations remain SMARCA4 loss -> stronger SMARCA2 dependency, KEAP1 loss -> weaker NFE2L2 dependency, MSI-high -> stronger WRN dependency, and TP53 wild-type -> stronger USP7 dependency. High screen_nnmd direct effects H155-H158 further support robustness across a screen-quality axis; H155-H157 are closed subset replications, and H158 is being independently validated because USP7 has smaller context-specific magnitude. Aneuploidy interactions for SMARCA2, NFE2L2, and WRN (H159-H161) are rejected as uninformative context modification; USP7 aneuploidy interaction (H162) remains unresolved due to wider uncertainty. Pathway-bypass RTK/MAPK claims H163-H166 are rejected as small/near-null in this dataset. Next, await H158 validation and decide whether lower screen_nnmd subsets or targeted interaction refinements are worth additional analysis; avoid additional low-prior marker screening unless motivated by an emerging signal. All statements remain dataset-internal associations, not causal or externally generalizable claims."
